  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Diaspora research: laying the groundwork for diaspora engagement in the Federated States of Micronesia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since 1986, citizens of the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) have been able to travel to and reside in the United States visa-free, contributing to significant outward migration for employment, education, family reunification, and healthcare. While migration has created opportunities, it has also resulted in the loss of skills and human capital, highlighting the need for stronger engagement with nationals abroad. Recognizing diaspora engagement as a key development priority in its Strategic Development Plan 2024-2043, the Government of FSM seeks to strengthen its understanding of diaspora communities, particularly in the United States. This project aims to support a structured approach to leveraging the human, social, economic, and cultural capital of the diaspora to contribute to the sustainable development of FSM. The project will generate up-to-date, gender-sensitive evidence on diaspora communities and the national ecosystem for diaspora engagement, while strengthening the capacity of government counterparts to utilize this information in the design and implementation of diaspora engagement strategies and initiatives.</narrative>
  </description>
